WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.880
Support vector machines is often referred
to as SVM for short.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.650
Support vector machines is a classifier that
finds an optimal hyperplane that maximizes

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.449
the margin between two classes.

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:22.939
Let's start with a very basic example with
only two dimensions.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:28.030
Using X1 and X2, we want a line to separate
the red squares from the blue circles.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:32.850
So, any line shown on this plot is really
a viable option.

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:36.670
They would all perfectly separate the red
squares from the blue circles.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:41.570
However, there has to be an optimal line,
or decision boundary.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.500
&nbsp;
In other words, what line would give us the

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.050
best chance to correctly classify additional
examples in each of these two classes?

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.090
You would probably want a line that is evenly
spaced between these two classes to give a

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.190
little bit of buffer for each class.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.609
That's exactly what support vector machines
does.

00:00:59.609 --> 00:01:03.980
The definition sounds fancy, but really, you're
just trying to find a line that's right in

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:08.030
the middle of your two classes, evenly spaced
between the two.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:12.660
Mathematically, it's defined as maximizing
the margin between your decision boundary

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:14.270
and the closest points.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.080
&nbsp;
Now, you might be wondering where this support

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.860
vector terminology comes from.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.090
Support vector is the name for the perpendicular
line from your decision boundary to your closest

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.470
points in both classes.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:30.690
On the right, it's the perpendicular line
that goes from that green optimal hyperplane

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.360
to those filled-in points, that filled-in
blue circle and the filled-in red squares.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.040
The goal is to maximize the length of those
support vectors.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.250
&nbsp;
In the example on the left, visually we can

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.210
see that non of these is the ideal decision
boundary.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.710
You can see how close these closest points
in each class are to the decision boundary,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:54.800
so the algorithm wouldn't like these as the
optimal solution.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.640
What SVM is seeking to do, is maximize that
distance between the decision boundary and

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.540
the nearest points.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.570
&nbsp;
In our definition, we talked about finding

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.890
an optimal hyperplane.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.190
In our examples, we've just been using a line
in 2 dimensional space.

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:15.340
So, a hyperplane is just a generalized term
to identify your decision boundary in an n

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:16.780
dimensional space.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.750
So, in two dimensions, that's just a line.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.670
In 3 dimension, it is a plane and so on.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:29.349
Up to this point, everything we've discussed
requires our data to be linearly separable,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:33.730
so we're using a straight line to separate
out examples in two-dimensional space, and

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.689
we are using a flat hyperplane to separate
examples in a three-dimensional space.

00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:42.099
We're not using any curved lines or curved
hyperplanes.

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:48.670
So, what happens when we have data that can't
be separated by a straight line or a hyperplane?

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:52.810
That leads us to this really creative trick
called the kernel trick.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.439
This transform our data which is not linearly
separable in n-dimensional space into a higher

00:02:57.439 --> 00:03:00.529
dimension where it is linearly separable.

00:03:00.529 --> 00:03:02.500
&nbsp;
Let's look at an example Here, we're trying

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.239
to identify the presence of cancer based on
gene X and gene Y.

00:03:07.239 --> 00:03:11.680
This data clearly is not linearly separable
in two dimensions.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.140
We couldn't draw a straight line here that
would separate the blue from the red.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:18.769
We would need a circle in order to do that.

00:03:18.769 --> 00:03:23.200
However, if we were to project this data from
two-dimensional space into three-dimensional

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.079
space like this, all of a sudden now we can
use a flat hyperplane to split our data very

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:30.889
nicely to identify the presence of cancer.

00:03:30.889 --> 00:03:37.780
So, that's the kernel trick, and it's a really
powerful tool for support vector machines.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.200
When should you consider using SVM?

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:46.189
SVM can be used for both categorical and continuous
outputs, but it is not be very useful for

00:03:46.189 --> 00:03:47.760
regression.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.709
It should only be a focus when you're working
on a classification problem with some kind

00:03:51.709 --> 00:03:54.409
of binary target variable.

00:03:54.409 --> 00:04:00.329
One area where SVM really excels is when the
feature-to-row ratio is very high.

00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:04.010
This is what we called short and fat data
previously.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.989
It's when you have a lot of features and relatively
few rows.

00:04:07.989 --> 00:04:13.030
Many algorithms will really suffer from these,
but SVM tends to do fairly well, and it's

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.840
actually one of its distinguishing features.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.329
SVM is also quite good at untangling complex
relationships.

00:04:20.329 --> 00:04:25.520
SVM also handles data with a lot of outliers
quite well.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:30.390
If you think about how SVM works using these
support vectors it actually makes sense, because

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:35.410
it only looks at the points closest to the
line, so that outliers are kind of ignored.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:40.320
So, if you're working with data with a lot
of outliers, and many other algorithms are

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.870
struggling, you might want to consider SVM.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.870
&nbsp;
When should you not use it?

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.010
You should not use it if you have a lot of
rows and relatively few features.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:55.230
Typically, SVM will take a long time to train,
and the performance really won't be worth

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:58.020
it relative to other algorithms.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.850
Despite the really nice plots that we saw
before, when you’re dealing with hundreds

00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:06.600
of features, and the hyperplane is an extremely
high-dimensional space, you can't really get

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:09.480
a good picture what's going on inside of SVM.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.480
So, if you care about transparency, and maybe
the significance of predictors, you would

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.650
probably avoid SVM.

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:19.420
&nbsp;
Lastly, if you're looking for a quick benchmark

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:23.130
model, SVM probably is not the right choice.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:28.180
SVM take quite a long time to train, and it
takes longer than other algorithms to make

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.340
predictions as well.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:36.550
You really want to avoid SVM if time or maybe
compute power is a significant constraint.

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.880
&nbsp;
In summary, SVM should be used when you have

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.440
a lot of features, but few rows, or when you
have a lot of outliers, that you're trying

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.190
to untangle.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.860
But it is quite slow to train, you might want
to look somewhere else.

